+# Answer: `fs.rmSync` options in Node 22
+
+## Documented options
+
+Source: Node.js v22 docs, `fs.rmSync(path[, options])`
+(https://nodejs.org/docs/latest-v22.x/api/fs.html#fsrmsyncpath-options).
+
+| Option | Type | Default | Meaning |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `force` | boolean | `false` | If `true`, no error is thrown when `path` does not exist. |
+| `maxRetries` | integer | `0` | How many times to retry after an `EBUSY`, `EMFILE`, `ENFILE`, `ENOTEMPTY` or `EPERM` error. Each retry waits `retryDelay` ms longer than the one before (linear backoff). Ignored unless `recursive: true`. |
+| `recursive` | boolean | `false` | If `true`, removes the directory and everything inside it. In recursive mode, failed operations are retried. |
+| `retryDelay` | integer | `100` | Milliseconds to wait between retries. Ignored unless `recursive: true`. |
+
+## The fix: `recursive: true`
+
+```js
+fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true });              // deletes the folder and its contents
+fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true, force: true }); // same, and no error if dir is missing (like `rm -rf`)
+```
+
+`force` alone does **not** fix this. It only stops errors for paths that don't exist.
+
+## Checked on Node v22.23.4
+
+- `fs.rmSync(dir)` on a non-empty directory → throws `ERR_FS_EISDIR`.
+- `fs.rmSync(dir)` on an **empty** directory → also throws `ERR_FS_EISDIR` ("Path is a directory: rm returned EISDIR"). Without `recursive`, `rmSync` refuses any directory, empty or not. The problem is not specific to non-empty folders.
+- `fs.rmSync(dir, { force: true })` → still throws `ERR_FS_EISDIR`.
+- `fs.rmSync(dir, { recursive: true })` → succeeds, and the directory is gone.
+- `fs.rmSync(missing)` → throws `ENOENT`. `fs.rmSync(missing, { force: true })` → succeeds without error.
+
+(To remove only an empty directory, `fs.rmdirSync(dir)` also works.)
